Body:
I looked into this and can confirm the root cause.

## Root cause

The migration code in `packages/desktop/src/main/migrate.ts` uses the old `.dat` filename directly as the electron-store name:

```ts
// migrate.ts:51
const storeName = filename === "opencode.settings.dat" ? "opencode.settings" : filename
```

So `opencode.workspace.Qzpc.vtnkm7.dat` gets migrated into a store named `opencode.workspace.Qzpc.vtnkm7.dat`.

But the app never reads that store. The workspace store name is generated in `packages/app/src/utils/persist.ts` using a completely different scheme:

```ts
// persist.ts:337-341
function workspaceStorage(dir: string) {
  const head = (dir.slice(0, 12) || "workspace").replace(/[^a-zA-Z0-9._-]/g, "-")
  const sum = checksum(dir) ?? "0"
  return `opencode.workspace.${head}.${sum}.dat`
}
// called with pathKey(dir), e.g. "C:/" → head="C--", giving "opencode.workspace.C--.hmddcc.dat"
```

The old Tauri app encoded the workspace path as base64 in the filename (`Qzpc` = base64 of `C:\`). The new Electron app uses `pathKey(dir)` + FNV checksum. These two schemes produce different filenames for the same workspace, so the migrated data lands in a store the app never opens.

The session keys (`session:ses_xxx:prompt`, etc.) appear in `skipped` in the log because the migration ran against the correct old file, but wrote into the wrong target store — and on a second run the `tauriMigrated` flag prevents it from running again.

## Fix

The migration needs to decode the old base64 workspace filename back to a path, then remap it to the new `workspaceStorage(pathKey(path))` name before writing. Something like:

```ts
// In migrateFile(), before getStore():
let storeName = filename === "opencode.settings.dat" ? "opencode.settings" : filename

// Remap old base64-encoded workspace filenames to the new format
const workspaceMatch = filename.match(/^opencode\.workspace\.([^.]+)\.[^.]+\.dat$/)
if (workspaceMatch) {
  try {
    const decoded = base64Decode(workspaceMatch[1])  // "Qzpc" → "C:\"
    const key = pathKey(decoded)
    storeName = workspaceStorage(key)
  } catch {
    // not a base64 name, keep original
  }
}
```

This ensures the session data lands in the store the app actually reads.

For now I fixed it by installing opencode on my host system with this config

```
{
  "$schema": "https://opencode.ai/config.json",
  "provider": {
    "lmstudio": {
      "npm": "@ai-sdk/openai-compatible",
      "name": "LM Studio (local)",
      "options": {
        "baseURL": "http://<MY_SERVER>:1234/v1",
      },
      "models": {
        "qwen/qwen3.6-35b-a3b": {
          "name": "Qwen 3.6 35B",
        },
      },
    },
  },
  "model": "lmstudio/qwen/qwen3.6-35b-a3b",
}
```

and then using inside zed: 


